+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md - Why. The service is layered: `routes/` parse HTTP input,
+`services/` hold rules, `repositories/` hold all SQL. `createApp` wires an
+`OrderService(OrderRepository, Clock)` and a `CouponService(CouponRepository)`.
+The order service currently has no access to coupons, and the order repository
+has no write path for amounts. `node:sqlite` (`DatabaseSync`) is synchronous and
+supports `db.exec`/`prepare`/transactions via manual `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK`.
+Time must come from the injected `Clock`.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Add the apply-coupon behavior with all rules in the service layer.
+- Keep SQL (including the transaction) in repositories.
+- Guarantee atomicity and no side effects on rejection.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- No schema changes, no new dependencies, no changes to existing tests.
+- No coupon removal, stacking, or changes to other endpoints.
+
+## Decisions
+
+### Order service gains a coupon repository
+
+`OrderService` receives a `CouponRepository` in addition to `OrderRepository`
+and `Clock`, and `app.ts` passes the existing `couponRepo`. Rationale: the
+rules span orders and coupons, so the orchestrating service needs both. The
+coupon admin service is unrelated and stays unchanged.
+
+### Route is a thin parser
+
+`routes/orders.ts` registers `POST /orders/:id/apply-coupon`, delegating to
+`orders.applyCoupon(parseId(p.id), body.couponCode)`. It performs no validation
+or normalization. Because `body ?? {}` yields `{}`, the service sees `undefined`
+for a missing code.
+
+### Normalization in the service
+
+The service rejects non-string / blank codes (`INVALID_REQUEST`), then trims and
+upper-cases before lookup, satisfying REQ-01/REQ-13. Normalization lives in the
+service so the rule is testable without HTTP.
+
+### Validation order mirrors the error table
+
+The service evaluates checks in the exact order of section 6 (REQ-13, order
+exists, order pending, already applied, coupon exists, active, not-yet-valid,
+expired, usage-exhausted, minimum-not-met) and throws the first matching
+`AppError` with the documented status/code. Time comparisons use `this.clock()`
+converted to epoch milliseconds against `Date.parse(validFrom)` /
+`Date.parse(validUntil)`, so `now >= validUntil` expires while `now ==
+validFrom` passes.
+
+### Discount math is pure and ordered
+
+Base discount: `FIXED` → `value`; `PERCENT` → `Math.floor(amount * value / 100)`.
+Then apply the cap (`min` with `maxDiscountCents` when non-NULL), then clamp to
+`amountCents`; `finalAmountCents = amountCents - discount`. This ordering is
+required so REQ-08 applies to both types before REQ-09 clamping.
+
+### Single atomic write in the repository
+
+The order repository exposes one method, e.g.
+`applyCoupon({ orderId, couponCode, discountCents, finalAmountCents, now })`,
+that wraps three statements in a transaction using `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` ...
+`COMMIT`, with `ROLLBACK` in a `catch` before rethrowing. It updates the order
+row, inserts the redemption row, and runs
+`UPDATE coupons SET used_count = used_count + 1 WHERE code = ?`. Rationale: one
+SQL boundary keeps the invariant (REQ-12) enforceable and rollback-able, and
+keeps SQL out of the service. Alternative considered: separate repository
+calls per table orchestrated in the service - rejected because atomicity would
+then depend on service control flow and split SQL ownership.
+
+The service then re-reads the order and returns it, so the 200 body is
+byte-for-byte what `GET /orders/:id` produces (REQ-14).
+
+### No schema or dependency changes
+
+The existing tables already carry all needed columns, and `DatabaseSync` gives
+transactions without a new library.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- **Concurrency on `used_count`** → the read-check and the increment are
+  separate statements, so a concurrent redemption could overshoot a limit under
+  true parallel writes. Acceptable here: `DatabaseSync` is synchronous and the
+  app is single-process; the transaction keeps the write atomic. Could be
+  tightened later with a conditional increment if needed.
+- **`Math.floor` on large percent math** → JS number arithmetic is safe for the
+  cents ranges in scope; values are validated as integers on coupon creation.
+- **Rejection paths must not write** → all validation happens before the
+  repository call, so a throw leaves the database untouched.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Purely additive: a new route and service method, no data migration. Rollback is
+reverting the code; existing data is unaffected.
